WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:04.080
Now some of you may think that I'm exaggerating

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.600
when I say this November's elections are more important

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.880
than any I can remember in my lifetime.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:14.880
In this election, we are fighting to win and
we are going to win.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.200
Republicans and Democrats may genuinely hate
each other

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.500
but they love their own power even more.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:26.280
That's why they collude so often to keep those
pesky Libertarians, Greens and Independents

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.900
away from the lucrative levers of government.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.480
The 2018 midterms, like all campaign seasons,

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.400
have been chock full of two party shenanigans.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:40.580
All too often aided and abetted by journalists,
pollsters and the voters themselves.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:42.680
It all starts with ballot access.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:47.140
If a state government considers you a major
party, getting on the ballot is a snap.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.140
Worst case scenario, you need to collect signatures

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:52.330
from a tiny fraction of your own registered
voter base.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:53.700
Best case you just show up-

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:54.820
How you doing?

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:56.460
-you're a major, party on.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.960
Third party and independent candidates, on
the other hand,

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.300
have to collect tens of thousands of signatures
in some states.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.880
Fifteen thousand to run for governor of New
York

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:07.800
including at least a hundred in each congressional
district.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.840
In fact, Arizona Republicans recently changed
the law

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.920
to make Libertarians have to collect signatures
not just from their own voters

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.400
but from registered independents as well.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.850
And by the way, that doesn't apply to the
Green party,

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:23.490
which means that in Arizona this year, in
the US Senate race which is neck and neck

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.760
there's a Green candidate but not a Libertarian.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.780
Some states push their filing deadlines all
the way back to a year before the election.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.720
That's one of many reasons why candidates
like Evan McMullen,

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.000
when they get a sudden itch to run for President,

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.620
are lucky to make it on even a dozen ballots.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:41.500
New Hampshire this year herded all third parties
under the same ballot line

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.900
confusingly titled Libertarians and other
candidates.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:49.340
New Mexico tried and thankfully failed to
institute a straight party ballot

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.090
meaning voters in this 3-2 Democratic state

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:57.560
could automatically vote for all the candidates
in one party by checking off just one box.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:01.820
Gary Johnson's surging poll numbers I am sure
were purely coincidental.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:05.600
Getting your name on the ballot doesn't mean
it's going to be included in the polls.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.720
The Nevada race for US Senate for example
is universally rated a toss up

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:13.220
and yet the first three independent polls
released this October

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:15.700
did not include Libertarian Tim Hagan

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:18.760
even though previous surveys had him at about
4%.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.180
How about the debates?

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:25.760
Pennsylvania Senate candidate Dale Kerns was
assured of a spot on stage and then uninvited.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:29.440
If we can implement fresh ideas into the system,
they're going to have to change.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.580
Texas gubernatorial candidate Mark Tippetts
was told he didn't have enough qualifying press

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:40.160
in part because his coverage in the Spanish
language media didn't count... in Texas.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:45.080
All Texans have a right to hear from all of
the options

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:48.060
that will be on the ballot for governor this November.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:53.240
Iowa governor candidate Jake Porter, who's
polling higher than any other LP statehouse contender,

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.820
says his debate invitations were rescinded,

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:58.540
in part because he refused to buy commercials.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:03.220
'The freer and more general the competition'
Adam Smith wrote in 1776

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.080
'the more advantageous it will be to the public.'

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:10.640
Competitors, he warned, will always try to
widen the market and narrow the competition.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:14.820
Our system of government, that other glorious
achievement from 1776

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:18.940
has for too long been controlled by the same
type of market rigging forces

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:21.000
that Adam Smith warned us about.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.560
It's about time to make American politics
competitive again.

